<p>The United States is building up military forces around Iran as negotiations over Iran’s nuclear program continue. Reporting describes a large deployment of aircraft carriers, fighter jets, surveillance planes, warships, and missile systems across regional bases and nearby seas under President Trump. Multiple outlets say the goal is to give the White House credible military options if diplomacy fails, and one report says some US strike planning has weighed targeting Iranian leadership and potentially seeking regime change. Politico reports Trump has not made a final decision on whether to strike or which targets to hit, even as he warned of “speed and violence” if talks with Tehran do not halt nuclear enrichment. The buildup is also drawing scrutiny at home over costs: extended deployments—paired with the Pentagon’s January operation aimed at ousting former Venezuelan leader Nicolás Maduro—could run into the billions and strain military budgets, potentially complicating the administration’s domestic affordability message ahead of midterms. Alongside official deployments, open-source clues are fueling speculation about what comes next. Le Monde says satellite imagery shows an unusually large US presence in the region, while Firstpost points to social-media footage of F-22 arrivals at Crete’s Chania Airport and says the US Army briefly posted, then removed, images of its “Dark Eagle” hypersonic missile.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/tPLKeYrGSk28S0Wp3HyLcaYPFvNd_UTH4KvdeCxfRiotjz8lItZftExWq94Ij77Rm3jMVvCQfDyo6sLCsa5VBWUzweLU5TJ590w8BgezYLvjAhnXQuKXBmsihiVIpn4qjDsmstnSHsSeL8GP8ItWIOIZAeQh9_wHRSVqLhFzjxtdAXic' alt='Satellite-view imagery used by Le Monde to document the US military buildup around Iran.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Le Monde visuals: Le Monde published an image-led look at the buildup, calling it an unprecedented deployment happening as nuclear negotiations continue.</li><li>Scale framing: Politico describes the posture as the biggest accumulation of American firepower in the region since the Iraq War.</li><li>Political risk at home: Politico says the operation’s cost marks a shift from Trump’s “America First” themes and could undercut his affordability message ahead of midterms.</li><li>Democratic critique: Sen. Ruben Gallego says voters will question the administration’s quick pivot from the January Venezuela operation to Iran, especially amid affordability concerns.</li><li>Regime-change option: NDTV says some strike options under discussion could include targeting Iranian leaders and pursuing regime change if diplomacy fails.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: People familiar with the planning say he has not made a final decision on whether to strike Iran or which targets to hit, even as the military buildup continues during nuclear talks. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/02/20/trumps-iran-buildup-risks-drowning-out-affordability-message-00788875'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Sen. Ruben Gallego (D-Ariz.): He criticized the administration’s shift from the January Venezuela operation to Iran, saying voters will question the focus given affordability concerns at home. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/02/20/trumps-iran-buildup-risks-drowning-out-affordability-message-00788875'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Le Monde analysis: The outlet says its satellite and open-source review shows a buildup around Iran that is unusually large for a moment when nuclear negotiations are still underway. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/21/us-military-buildup-around-iran-what-satellite-images-show_6750733_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-strikes-on-iran-could-target-leaders-seek-regime-change-report-11119697#publisher=newsstand'>US Strikes On Iran Could Target Leaders, Seek Regime Change: Report</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/21/us-